# Structured merging via Mergiraf (https://mergiraf.org).
#
# Git merges by line, which means a change that MOVES a line — a reindent, a
# reformat, a method shifted up or down — conflicts with any concurrent edit to
# that line, even when the two changes are unrelated. Mergiraf parses the file
# and merges by syntax tree instead, so it can see that the moved line and the
# edited line are the same line.
#
# It is a hybrid, not a replacement: git's own line merge runs FIRST and
# Mergiraf is invoked only on the hunks that conflict. It therefore cannot turn
# a clean merge into a wrong one — it only ever converts a conflict into a
# resolution, and falls back to git's conflict markers when the merge is
# genuinely ambiguous (both sides inserting a sibling at the same position, for
# instance, where the resulting order would be arbitrary).
#
# Inspect any resolution it made with:  mergiraf review <id>
#
# To enable it, install mergiraf and configure the driver once per clone:
#
#     git config merge.mergiraf.driver \
#         'mergiraf merge --git %O %A %B -s %S -x %X -y %Y -p %P'
#
# A clone WITHOUT any mergiraf config is unaffected: git does not recognise the
# driver name and silently uses its ordinary three-way merge (verified — it
# conflicts exactly as it would with no .gitattributes at all). Nothing here is
# required in order to build or contribute.
#
# Only the extensions Gryphon actually tracks and Mergiraf actually supports are
# listed. Run `mergiraf languages --gitattributes` for the full set.

*.scala      merge=mergiraf
*.sbt        merge=mergiraf
*.java       merge=mergiraf
*.md         merge=mergiraf
*.yml        merge=mergiraf
*.yaml       merge=mergiraf
*.html       merge=mergiraf
*.properties merge=mergiraf
